# Mutation cases for the generated code catalog in SCE_ERROR_CONTRACT.md §5.
#
# Run with `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/error_contract_catalog.cases`.
#
# What these guard is a document that PROMISED completeness and was never
# read. Measured 2026-08-12: §5 opened with "The full enumeration of `code`
# values" and named 96 of 343, fifteen stages absent, while the sibling gates
# over `docs/SCE_ACCEPTED_SUBSET.md` and `SCE_MESH.md` stayed green. A claim
# with no reader is the failure mode here, so every case below restores a
# shape in which the claim is false and nothing says so.
#
# Both targets are compiled into the test binary — the document is pulled in
# with `include_str!` and the generator is Rust — so they are ordinary
# `mutation_targets`, not runtime ones.
#
# Anchors are literal slices of the source. A reformat can move one out from
# under a case; that is reported as INCONCLUSIVE with the anchor quoted, never
# as a pass.

mutation_tests -p sce-build --features cli --lib
mutation_targets sce-build/src/forge/diagnostic.rs SCE_ERROR_CONTRACT.md
# `--lib` names no test target, so nothing here is derivable from the selector.
# Measured instead, out of the round's own record: five of the six cases red
# `forge::diagnostic::tests::error_contract_catalog_is_the_full_enumeration`,
# and the sixth reds `contract_docs_cite_only_real_codes` — the wire-contract
# case is aimed at the document rather than at the catalog, so it earns its
# verdict from a different assertion. That module is the file already under
# study, so the two sides of the verdict happen to share a file here, which is
# why this casefile was never exposed to the gap the declaration closes.
#
# ⚠ This sentence said "all four cases" until 2026-08-26, and the file held SIX
# on the day that landed — it was wrong on arrival, not stale by drift. It read
# as true for nine days because nothing in the harness reads prose and every
# case was CAUGHT. `mutation_casefile_headers_count_their_cases` now counts the
# number back.
mutation_oracles sce-build/src/forge/diagnostic.rs

# ── The catalog is complete ────────────────────────────────────────

mutation_case "a whole pipeline is dropped from the catalog" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/src/forge/diagnostic.rs",
     '            mesh_golden_entries().iter().map(|(_, e, _)| single(e)),\n            &mut mesh,',
     '            std::iter::empty(),\n            &mut mesh,')
PY

mutation_case "one code is filtered out of the catalog" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/src/forge/diagnostic.rs",
     "        for (code, row) in rows {",
     "        for (code, row) in rows.iter().filter(|(c, _)| **c != \"io/filesystem\") {")
PY

# The completeness check guards the REFRESH path, so a case that only
# deletes it proves nothing: measured, that mutation SURVIVED, and rightly
# — while the generator is healthy the round-trip comparison is sufficient
# on its own, and removing a net changes nothing until something falls.
# The case has to be the composite the check exists for: a generator that
# drops a code, and a run that takes the path which would write it.
mutation_case "a refresh writes a catalog that is missing a code" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/src/forge/diagnostic.rs",
     "        for (code, row) in rows {",
     "        for (code, row) in rows.iter().filter(|(c, _)| **c != \"io/filesystem\") {")
edit("sce-build/src/forge/diagnostic.rs",
     'if std::env::var_os("UPDATE_EXPECT").is_some() {',
     "if true {")
PY

# ── The document matches what the producer emits ───────────────────

mutation_case "a row in the checked-in catalog is edited by hand" <<'PY'
edit("SCE_ERROR_CONTRACT.md",
     "| `io/filesystem` | `io` | no |",
     "| `io/filesystem` | `xml` | no |")
PY

mutation_case "a row is deleted from the checked-in catalog" <<'PY'
import re
p = "SCE_ERROR_CONTRACT.md"
src = open(p).read()
row = re.search(r"^\| `cli/unknown-language` \|.*\n", src, re.M).group(0)
edit(p, row, "")
PY

# ── The prose cites only codes that exist ──────────────────────────

mutation_case "the wire contract quotes a code SCE does not emit" <<'PY'
edit("SCE_ERROR_CONTRACT.md",
     "## 6. Exit codes",
     "Consumers branch on `validation/invented-for-this-mutation`.\n\n## 6. Exit codes")
PY
